---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import '../../styles/pages/inside-sp-plus.css';

/*
 * Inside SP+ -- the reference document, brought into the site.
 *
 * This arrived as a standalone HTML file carrying its own palette and two
 * Google font links. Both have been removed: the page now runs on tokens.css
 * and the site's own faces, inside the standard Layout, so it inherits the
 * nav, the footer, the share card and the view transitions.
 *
 * It is deliberately not behind the member gate. The document exists to be read
 * before somebody talks to us, and the closing section says plainly that we
 * hand the deeper report out on request rather than on qualification. It is
 * noindex, so it is a link you give somebody rather than a page they find.
 */

const sections = [
  ['s1', 'The plain statement'],
  ['s2', 'Who it is for, and who it is not for'],
  ['s3', 'The four things it exists to do'],
  ['s4', 'What actually changes, against the laptop you have now'],
  ['s5', 'What is actually on the machine'],
  ['s6', 'The protections, one layer at a time'],
  ['s7', 'How we know any of this is true'],
  ['s8', 'What SP+ deliberately does not do'],
  ['s9', 'What is still true after all of it'],
  ['s10', 'A day with it'],
  ['s11', 'Questions advisors actually ask'],
  ['s12', 'Things we will never say to you'],
];
---

<Layout
  title="Inside SP+: SecureProspective"
  description="A complete account of SP+: what it protects, how every protection was tested, and the places where it stops."
  noindex
>
  <Nav />

  <main id="main" class="inside-page" transition:name="page-card">
    <header class="inside-masthead">
      <div class="inside-shell">
        <p class="inside-eyebrow">SP+ // Reference edition 0.11</p>
        <h1>Inside SP+</h1>
        <p class="inside-deck">
          The whole account of the computer we give to advisors: what it protects, how every
          protection was measured on a real machine, and the places where it stops. Written to be
          read before you talk to anyone, including the parts that are unflattering.
        </p>
        <div class="inside-figures">
          <div><strong>$0</strong><span>To eligible members. No tier, no seat count.</span></div>
          <div><strong>154</strong><span>Checks, each proven capable of failing.</span></div>
          <div><strong>2,004</strong><span>Components, inventoried inside the image.</span></div>
          <div><strong>12</strong><span>Of our own tests that tested nothing, published.</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="inside-actions">
          <button type="button" class="btn" id="print-btn">Print or save as PDF</button>
          <p class="inside-note-text">
            Measured on an installed machine, 11 September 2026. Fedora KDE lane.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="inside-shell inside-glance">
      <div class="inside-glance-card">
        <div class="inside-glance-head">
          <span>Ninety seconds, if that is all you have</span>
          <span>The four outcomes</span>
        </div>
        <div class="inside-glance-grid">
          <div>
            <span class="inside-glance-n">01</span>
            <h2>A stolen laptop is an insurance claim</h2>
            <p>
              The whole disk is encrypted from the first minute. Not a setting, not a checkbox,
              and not something you can forget to switch on.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="inside-glance-n">02</span>
            <h2>You can answer the security questionnaire</h2>
            <p>
              One documented configuration, identical on every SP+ machine, that the machine can
              describe in plain language instead of you answering from memory.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="inside-glance-n">03</span>
            <h2>It does not wear out, and it explains itself</h2>
            <p>
              System files cannot be written to while it runs, updates apply whole or not at all,
              and the previous version stays on the disk.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="inside-glance-n">04</span>
            <h2>It costs nothing and your day does not change</h2>
            <p>
              Free to eligible members. The same browser, the same carrier portals, the same CRM.
              Everything underneath is different. Almost nothing on top of it is.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="inside-shell">
      <nav class="inside-toc" aria-label="Contents">
        <h2>What is in here</h2>
        <ol>
          {
            sections.map(([id, label], i) => (
              <li>
                <a href={`#${id}`}>
                  <span class="inside-toc-n">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
                  <span>{label}</span>
                </a>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <section class="inside-card" id="s1" aria-labelledby="s1-title">
        <div class="inside-card-bar"><h2 id="s1-title">The plain statement</h2><span>01</span></div>
        <div class="inside-body">
          <p class="lead">
            SP+ is a complete computer system, free to eligible SecureProspective members, built for
            one job: making the laptop your client records live on hard to compromise, hard to break,
            and easy to account for. It arrives already set up. There is nothing to configure and no
            command line, and if you can use a web browser you can use it.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is not Windows and it is not a Mac, but it is not meant to feel like a change of
            career either. You get a familiar desktop, a familiar browser, and the same websites you
            already work in: your carrier portals, your CRM, e-signature, quoting, Google Workspace
            or Microsoft 365, video calls. One edition is laid out like Windows, another like a Mac.
            You pick by answering one question, and we never guess from your hardware.
          </p>

          <div class="pull">
            Everything underneath is different. Almost nothing on top of it is. That is the design
            goal, not a shortcoming.
          </div>

          <p>
            This document goes further than a product page would, on purpose. An advisor deciding
            whether to put their practice on an unfamiliar computer deserves the real account,
            including the parts that are unflattering. Section 8 lists protections we chose not to
            build and why. Section 9 lists what remains risky after everything we did build. Section
            7 explains how each claim in between was tested, and includes a register of our own
            tests that failed to test anything.
          </p>

          <div class="note">
            <span class="tag">The one paragraph everything here must survive</span>
            <p>
              SP+ protects client information using strong encryption and careful defaults, and it
              produces records you can show to anyone who asks about your security practices. It does
              not make you compliant with any law or regulation, it does not replace your own
              responsibility for protecting client data, and it does not promise that every printer,
              camera, or gadget will work. It helps you do the right things and prove you did them.
              The rest is up to you, as it always has been.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="inside-card" id="s2" aria-labelledby="s2-title">
        <div class="inside-card-bar"><h2 id="s2-title">Who it is for, and who it is not for</h2><span>02</span></div>
        <div class="inside-body">
          <p>
            SP+ was built for one person in particular, and every decision in it was made for that
            person rather than for a general audience.
          </p>

          <h3>The advisor we built it for</h3>
          <p>
            An independent insurance or financial advisor running a one-person business, or a
            principal with a member of staff or two. A career built on relationships rather than
            technology. Almost certainly on Windows today. Working nearly all day inside a browser.
            And holding, on an ordinary laptop, some of the most sensitive information in civilian
            commerce: names, dates of birth, Social Security numbers, health disclosures from life
            and long-term care applications, bank and routing numbers for premium drafts, beneficiary
            designations, income and asset statements. Usually sitting in a downloads folder, in
            plain form, on a disk that is not encrypted.
          </p>

          <p>
            Behind that advisor there is no IT department, no managed service provider, no device
            management, and no written security program. When something breaks, the options today
            are a relative, a local shop, or nobody. Tolerance for disruption is close to zero,
            because a day lost to a computer problem is a day of lost commission.
          </p>

          <p>Two things follow from that, and they run through every page of this document.</p>

          <div class="grid2">
            <div class="cell">
              <h4>A protection that breaks your workday is worse than no protection</h4>
              <p>
                Anything that breaks printing, printer discovery, Wi-Fi, the browser, your web apps,
                sound, camera, microphone, sleep and wake, a second monitor, or Bluetooth is rejected
                outright, however strong it is. An advisor who cannot print reinstalls Windows, and
                then the protection guards nothing at all.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="cell">
              <h4>There is nobody to absorb complexity for you</h4>
              <p>
                Anything that depends on a decision you have no way to make is not a security
                feature. That is why the password rule sets a length instead of demanding symbols,
                why a lockout clears itself in two minutes instead of needing an administrator, and
                why several choices in section 6 look unusual until you read the reason.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3>Who it is not for, stated before you spend an evening on it</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Anyone with a Windows-only desktop program they cannot work without</strong>
              and that has no web version. Illustration software, an old agency package, a carrier
              tool that only runs installed. SP+ replaces the operating system, so that program does
              not come with you.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Anyone who wants to tinker.</strong> The system is deliberately closed to
              modification. That is the point of it, and a technical user will find it restrictive.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Firms with a managed Windows fleet and a security team.</strong> They already
              have what this replaces.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>We would rather lose you at this paragraph than at the end of a difficult week.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="inside-card" id="s3" aria-labelledby="s3-title">
        <div class="inside-card-bar"><h2 id="s3-title">The four things it exists to do</h2><span>03</span></div>
        <div class="inside-body">
          <p>
            An advisor does not want an operating system. They want four outcomes, and SP+ was only
            worth building if it delivers all four.
          </p>

          <h3>1. If this laptop is stolen, I am not the subject of a breach notification</h3>
          <p>
            This is the strongest reason to do any of it. The whole disk is encrypted, and the
            encryption is not optional, not a setting, and not something you have to remember to turn
            on. A stolen encrypted laptop is an insurance claim for a laptop. A stolen unencrypted
            laptop is a legal event with notification costs, credit monitoring, and a conversation
            with every client whose file was on it.
          </p>

          <h3>2. I can answer the security questionnaire</h3>
          <p>
            Cyber insurance applications, carrier and IMO onboarding, broker-dealer supervision, RIA
            due diligence. These ask you to attest to your security posture, and most advisors answer
            from memory and hope. SP+ is built so the honest answer is a strong one, and so that the
            machine can describe its own state in plain language rather than leaving you to guess.
          </p>

          <h3>3. It does not break, and when it does, something explains it to me</h3>
          <p>
            The system files cannot be altered while the machine is running, so a bad install or a
            half-finished download cannot degrade it over time. Updates apply as a whole or not at
            all, and the previous version stays on the disk. A built-in assistant diagnoses problems
            in plain English and fixes them only after you say yes. This is not a philosophy. It is
            how the program survives without a support desk.
          </p>

          <h3>4. It costs nothing and my life did not change</h3>
          <p>
            Free to eligible members. No licence fee, no seat count, no premium tier, no upsell
            waiting behind it. Eligibility is checked once, when you download. It is never enforced
            inside the computer: if your membership status ever changes, your machine keeps working
            and keeps receiving updates. Building a licence check into someone's operating system
            would be both hostile and a liability.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="inside-card" id="s4" aria-labelledby="s4-title">
        <div class="inside-card-bar"><h2 id="s4-title">What actually changes, against the laptop you have now</h2><span>04</span></div>
        <div class="inside-body">
          <p class="lead">
            Everything in this document is a choice, and every one of those choices was made against
            the same reference point: the Windows laptop sitting on your desk right now.
          </p>

          <p>
            Windows is not a bad operating system. It is an operating system built on the assumption
            that somebody is administering the machine, keeping it patched, deciding what may be
            installed, and answering the questions it asks. In a firm with an IT department, that
            assumption holds. On the desk of a one-person practice, it does not, and the gap between
            what Windows expects of its administrator and what an advisor can actually do is where
            almost every real incident lives.
          </p>

          <p>
            That single difference explains most of the table below. Read the fourth column rather
            than the third: the reason is the argument, and the feature is only the consequence.
          </p>

          <div class="tablewrap">
            <table class="compare">
              <caption>The nine differences that matter to a practice</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">What</th>
                  <th scope="col">On the laptop you have now</th>
                  <th scope="col" class="us">On SP+</th>
                  <th scope="col">Why we made that choice</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>Encryption</td>
                  <td>
                    Available on most business editions and, in practice, very often never switched
                    on. On many consumer machines it is not offered at all, and where it is, the key
                    can end up held in an account rather than by you.
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    On from the first minute. Not a setting, not a checkbox, and not something you
                    can forget. The recovery key is generated on your own machine and has never been
                    seen by anyone here.
                  </td>
                  <td class="why">
                    It is the one control that turns a stolen laptop from a legal event into an
                    insurance claim. Anything optional ends up switched off, so this one is not
                    optional.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Hardware you already own</td>
                  <td>
                    A perfectly good laptop from a few years ago can be refused by the current
                    Windows over a hardware requirement, leaving you to choose between buying a
                    machine and running one that no longer receives security updates.
                  </td>
                  <td>Runs on that hardware and keeps receiving updates on it.</td>
                  <td class="why">
                    Nobody should have to buy a computer in order to become safer. A working machine
                    that is out of support is the most common way an advisor ends up exposed without
                    doing anything wrong.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Updates</td>
                  <td>
                    Applied into the running system, sometimes restarting you at the worst moment,
                    and when one goes wrong the way back is uncertain and usually involves somebody
                    technical.
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    A complete replacement copy is prepared quietly and finishes when you shut down.
                    The previous version stays on the disk and the machine can return to it.
                  </td>
                  <td class="why">
                    Recovering from a bad update should not depend on knowing what the update
                    changed. It should be a step backwards that always exists.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>What stops something bad</td>
                  <td>
                    Layers of detection, plus a stream of warnings that you learn to dismiss because
                    most of them are noise.
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    The system files cannot be written to while the machine runs, software arrives
                    from a vetted catalogue, and applications are boxed off from each other.
                  </td>
                  <td class="why">
                    Detection asks you to be right every time. Structure does not ask you anything.
                    Neither approach saves your documents from a compromised browser, which is stated
                    plainly in section 9.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Where software comes from</td>
                  <td>
                    Anything downloadable from the open web, including installers that arrive
                    carrying things you did not ask for.
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    A catalogue limited to verified publishers, plus a short list SecureProspective
                    vouches for by name. Adding a new source is a question the assistant puts to you.
                  </td>
                  <td class="why">
                    Most bad days begin with a download that looked right at the time. Narrowing
                    where software can come from removes the decision rather than asking you to make
                    it well.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Administrator prompts</td>
                  <td>
                    A prompt appears often enough that approving it becomes a reflex rather than a
                    decision.
                  </td>
                  <td>
                    No prompt at all, deliberately. Containment comes from the system being
                    unwritable and one step away from its previous state.
                  </td>
                  <td class="why">
                    A prompt that is always approved protects nobody and trains the habit that gets
                    people caught. This is the one row that is not a straightforward win, and it is
                    discussed below.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>When it breaks</td>
                  <td>A relative, a local shop, or nobody, and a working day lost while you wait.</td>
                  <td>
                    An assistant that reads the machine, explains what it found in your own words,
                    and fixes it once you say yes.
                  </td>
                  <td class="why">
                    A free operating system that generates the same support calls as Windows cannot
                    survive economically, which means it would not be here for you in three years.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>What it costs</td>
                  <td>The licence, an antivirus subscription, and eventually the replacement machine.</td>
                  <td>
                    Nothing. Free to eligible members, no seat count, no premium tier, no upsell
                    behind it.
                  </td>
                  <td class="why">
                    A benefit with a paid tier waiting behind it is a sales funnel. This is a member
                    benefit, and it is built to stay one.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Answering the questionnaire</td>
                  <td>From memory, and hopefully.</td>
                  <td>
                    One documented configuration that is the same on every SP+ machine, with this
                    document and the full architecture report behind it.
                  </td>
                  <td class="why">
                    You should be able to describe your own security setup accurately without
                    guessing, and without claiming more than is true.
                  </td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            One row in that table is not a win, and it would have been easy to write it as one. SP+
            asks for no administrative password at all. That is the right decision for a machine
            whose owner often cannot answer a password prompt and whose assistant has to be able to
            repair things without one, and it is genuinely the loss of a boundary rather than the
            removal of an annoyance. Section 8 records the decision and section 9 records what it
            costs you. We would rather you read that here than discover it later.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rest of this document is the detail underneath the table: what is on the machine, how
            each protection works, how it was tested, and where it stops.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="inside-card" id="s5" aria-labelledby="s5-title">
        <div class="inside-card-bar"><h2 id="s5-title">What is actually on the machine</h2><span>05</span></div>
        <div class="inside-body">
          <p>
            The figures below were read off a real installed machine on 11 September 2026, not copied
            from a plan. The technical names are included in small type for anyone you might forward
            this to.
          </p>

          <div class="ledger">
            <div class="row">
              <div class="k">The foundation</div>
              <div class="v">
                Fedora KDE, a mainstream and heavily reviewed Linux system, taken exactly as
                published and locked to one specific published version.
                <span class="tn">fedora-kinoite, pinned by digest</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="row">
              <div class="k">How it is delivered</div>
              <div class="v">
                The whole operating system is one sealed image. Your machine runs a copy of it rather
                than a collection of parts assembled on your desk.
                <span class="tn">bootc image mode</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="row">
              <div class="k">System files</div>
              <div class="v">
                Read-only while the machine is running. Not locked by permission, but genuinely not
                writable. <span class="tn">composefs, /usr read-only</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="row">
              <div class="k">The engine</div>
              <div class="v">
                Stock Fedora, unmodified. No custom parts and no third-party drivers bolted in, which
                is what keeps secure startup working with no menus to navigate.
                <span class="tn">kernel 7.1.13, no out-of-tree modules</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="row">
              <div class="k">Software inventory</div>
              <div class="v">
                2,004 components, every one of them listed inside the image itself so the machine can
                account for what it is made of.
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="row">
              <div class="k">Browser</div>
              <div class="v">Brave, locked to one exact version, configured before you ever open it.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="row">
              <div class="k">Internal separation</div>
              <div class="v">
                On and enforcing, which is the stronger of the two available settings.
                <span class="tn">SELinux enforcing</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="row">
              <div class="k">Disk</div>
              <div class="v">
                Encrypted with a modern, deliberately slow-to-guess scheme. Details in section 6.
                <span class="tn">LUKS2, argon2id</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="row">
              <div class="k">Updates</div>
              <div class="v">
                Prepared quietly on a daily schedule and finished when you shut down. The machine
                never restarts itself while you are working.
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="row">
              <div class="k">Where it is built</div>
              <div class="v">
                On one machine that SecureProspective owns and controls. That is a real limitation
                and it is named again in section 9.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Every build stamps its own identity inside the image it produces: the build date, the
            exact foundation version it came from, the browser version, the engine version, the
            component count. An installed machine can therefore say what it is without asking anyone.
            A build that fails to record that is refused and never becomes an image.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="inside-card" id="s6" aria-labelledby="s6-title">
        <div class="inside-card-bar"><h2 id="s6-title">The protections, one layer at a time</h2><span>06</span></div>
        <div class="inside-body">
          <p class="lead">
            This is the long section. Each layer says what it does, then says what it costs or where
            it stops, because a protection described without its limit is marketing.
          </p>

          <h3>The disk</h3>
          <p>
            The entire disk is encrypted, and it is unlocked by something only you know, entered once
            when the machine starts. The scheme is chosen to be expensive to attack: every guess is
            made to consume a large amount of memory and real time, tuned to your own machine when
            the disk is first encrypted, which is what defeats the racks of graphics cards that make
            ordinary password guessing cheap.
            <span class="tn">LUKS2, aes-xts-plain64, 512-bit key, argon2id</span>
          </p>
          <p>
            A recovery key is generated on your own machine the first time it starts, and never
            before, so nobody at SecureProspective has ever seen it. You are asked to write it down
            and store it away from the computer, in a safe or a lockbox.
          </p>
          <div class="note">
            <span class="tag">The cost, stated</span>
            <p>
              That recovery key is a second thing that opens your disk. Where you keep it decides
              whether the encryption still means what it says. Taped inside the laptop bag, it means
              nothing. In a safe at home, it means everything. This is the single most important
              thing you personally control.
            </p>
          </div>
          <p>
            The small startup area of the disk is not encrypted, which is normal and necessary. Its
            integrity is carried by secure startup instead.
          </p>

          <h3>Startup</h3>
          <p>
            SP+ uses the startup verification your laptop already shipped with, untouched, with the
            manufacturer's own keys. We add no keys of our own and ask you to approve nothing at a
            firmware screen. This is deliberate: every custom key is a screen that an advisor would
            have to navigate alone, in a menu that can render the machine unbootable. The trade is
            that we inherit trust in your hardware vendor, which is stated plainly in section 9.
            <span class="tn">Secure Boot, no MOK enrolment</span>
          </p>

          <h3>A system that cannot be worn down</h3>
          <p>
            On a normal computer, damage accumulates. Half-finished installers, a bad driver, an
            update that lands on top of a modified file, five years of well-meant changes. SP+
            removes that entire category.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>The system files cannot be written to while the machine runs.</strong> Not by
              you, not by a program, not by something malicious that gets in.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>An update is a complete new copy, prepared alongside the current one.</strong>
              It either applies whole or it does not apply. There is no half-updated state to be
              stranded in.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>The previous version stays on the disk.</strong> If a release ever misbehaves,
              the machine goes back to the one before it.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Updates never interrupt you.</strong> They are prepared on a daily schedule and
              finished when you shut down. Our build process actively refuses to produce an image
              whose updater is capable of restarting the machine on its own.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>There is one update path, not three.</strong> The ability to install system
              packages by hand has been removed from the software centre, so the machine cannot be
              led into a state its own updater cannot repair. The software centre keeps the things it
              should own: sandboxed applications and firmware.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>A daily health check</strong> can mark the system as needing attention rather
              than leaving a problem to be discovered later.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <div class="note">
            <span class="tag">The limit, stated</span>
            <p>
              Going back to the previous version is an undo for the system, not a backup of your
              work. It restores the operating system. It does not restore your files, and it does not
              protect you from ransomware or a failed disk. SP+ ships no backup system in this
              version. Keep your documents in cloud storage, on an external drive, or both. This is
              the most important gap in the product for an ordinary bad day.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h3>The browser, which is where your work actually happens</h3>
          <p>
            Brave arrives configured rather than merely installed, and the configuration is managed
            by the system rather than being a set of preferences you have to find.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Tracker and ad blocking is armed before the first page ever loads, not after you notice it.</li>
            <li>Known-dangerous site warnings are on and cannot be quietly switched off.</li>
            <li>
              The browser checks your saved passwords against known breaches and tells you when one
              of them has been exposed.
            </li>
            <li>
              Card details are never offered to be saved or filled in, so a carrier portal cannot
              autofill a payment card from the browser.
            </li>
            <li>
              Brave's built-in tip jar, wallet, news feed, video calling, VPN and AI chat are all
              switched off. They are not security features and each one is another thing to explain.
            </li>
            <li>
              Your business tools are set up as proper applications in the taskbar rather than as a
              wall of tabs, so the CRM is a thing you click and not a bookmark you hunt for.
            </li>
            <li>Only vetted extensions can run.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Additional applications come from a catalogue restricted to publisher-verified entries,
            plus a short list SecureProspective vouches for by name. Those applications run inside
            their own sandbox and reach the rest of the system only through controlled doors.
            <span class="tn">Flathub verified subset, Flatpak/bubblewrap, portals</span>
          </p>
          <div class="note">
            <span class="tag">The weakest link, named</span>
            <p>
              The browser itself is installed the traditional way rather than sandboxed like those
              applications, so it sits outside that particular boundary and relies on its own
              internal sandbox, which is strong but is not the same thing. On one laptop belonging to
              one non-technical person, the strongest available answer would be to run the browser
              inside a separate virtual machine, and that is not compatible with this user. We chose
              the user. Section 8 records the trigger that would reopen the decision.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h3>Signing in</h3>
          <p>
            The password rule asks for length, not punctuation. Twelve characters minimum, no
            requirement for a capital or a symbol or a digit. <code>harbour ledger spring</code> is
            accepted. <code>Passw0rd!</code> is not, because it is a dictionary word with predictable
            decoration. Passwords containing your own name or username are refused.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is not us being relaxed. Composition rules are the reason people write
            <code>Password1!</code> on a sticky note. Length is what actually resists guessing, and a
            passphrase is something a human can remember without a note.
            <span class="tn">pwquality minlen 12, no class credits</span>
          </p>
          <p>
            Ten wrong attempts in fifteen minutes locks the account, and the lock clears itself after
            two minutes. We specifically refuse to build an image where the lock is permanent or
            where it can lock out the administrator, because there is nobody for you to call and a
            mistyped password should never turn a working laptop into a paperweight.
          </p>

          <h3>The network you are sitting on</h3>
          <p>
            Hotel Wi-Fi, airport Wi-Fi, and the coffee shop are the everyday hostile network for an
            advisor who travels.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>The firewall is on and set to its untrusted profile by default.</li>
            <li>Remote access is installed but switched off, so the machine is not listening for anyone.</li>
            <li>
              Look-ups of website addresses are encrypted where the network allows it, so the
              operator of the cafe Wi-Fi does not get a readable list of the carrier sites you
              visited. <span class="tn">DNS over TLS, opportunistic</span>
            </li>
            <li>
              Printer discovery is left on. It is a small exposure on a shared network, and we accept
              it openly because a computer that cannot find a printer is a computer that gets
              replaced.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Address-lookup encryption is described as opportunistic for an honest reason: an attacker
            who controls the network can block the encrypted path and force it back to the ordinary
            one. It defeats the passive observer and it does not defeat an active one.
          </p>

          <h3>The assistant</h3>
          <p>
            SP+ includes an assistant called Fin. Fin's job is to take a sentence like "my printer
            stopped working" and actually fix it, rather than hand you a list of steps to type.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>It explains before it acts and asks before it changes anything.</strong> Nothing
              autonomous, and it tells you afterwards what happened, in your words.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>It cannot read your business.</strong> It works on the machine's own technical
              state. Your documents, email, browsing history, passwords, vault, and client records
              are not its material.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>What leaves the machine is limited by code, not by policy.</strong> When Fin
              needs online help to diagnose something, the request passes through a filter that
              permits only technical facts: hardware model, software versions, service status, error
              codes with personal details stripped, network state without credentials, printer
              status. Documents, email, calendar, contacts, CRM records, passwords, cookies,
              clipboard contents, client names, policy information and file names are blocked from
              leaving. A screenshot is sent only if you approve that individual screenshot.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Anything that would outlive the conversation gets shown to you.</strong> Added
              in this release: if a step would add a new source of software, install a signing key,
              create a service that starts by itself, add another administrator, or change where the
              machine gets its updates, Fin stops and shows you in plain words, and you can decline.
              Those are the changes that persist after the problem is solved, so they are the ones
              worth a question.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <div class="note">
            <span class="tag">The honest boundary</span>
            <p>
              That last confirmation is a check, not a wall. It is a strong defence against
              instructions hidden in something Fin reads, because text cannot answer a prompt. It is
              not a defence against malicious software already running as you, which could in
              principle answer the prompt itself. We tested that specifically rather than assuming
              it, and we describe the control as what it is. A true wall requires an approval that
              happens outside your session, and that is on the roadmap and not built.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h3>How the system itself is built and delivered</h3>
          <p>
            There is no golden machine that somebody configured by hand and photographed. The entire
            operating system is defined by a recipe held in version control, and every image is
            produced by running that recipe from the published Fedora foundation, locked to one exact
            version so that a build today and a build next week start from the same place.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Nothing is repackaged or rebuilt from source by us, so Fedora's own signing remains the
              chain of trust for the software you run.
            </li>
            <li>
              Images are signed with a key held on the build machine that has never left it. The
              matching public half ships inside the image so a machine can check what it is given.
            </li>
            <li>Every image carries its own inventory and provenance, described in section 5.</li>
            <li>
              Making verification mandatory on every installed machine is the current piece of work,
              and until it is finished end to end we describe it as being switched on rather than as
              done.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            We also trust a second software publisher directly, which is Brave, so that you get the
            browser the business runs on. That is a deliberate second dependency rather than an
            accident, and it is listed with the others in section 9.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="inside-card" id="s7" aria-labelledby="s7-title">
        <div class="inside-card-bar"><h2 id="s7-title">How we know any of this is true</h2><span>07</span></div>
        <div class="inside-body">
          <p class="lead">
            This is the part we would want to read first if somebody handed us this document. Every
            security product says it has encryption and updates and a firewall. The question is
            whether anyone checked, and whether the check was capable of failing.
          </p>

          <h3>Two independent layers of checking</h3>
          <p>
            SP+ carries 74 checks that run while the image is being built. Any one of them failing
            stops the build, so there is no way to produce an image that skipped one. They cover the
            things that can only be examined while the system is still being assembled.
          </p>
          <p>
            It then carries 80 further checks that run against a real machine that has actually been
            installed from the finished USB stick and started up for the first time. These measure
            effect rather than intent. A build check can confirm that a setting ships in the right
            file. Only a running machine can confirm that the setting is in force.
          </p>

          <h3>The rule that makes a check mean something</h3>
          <div class="pull">
            Before any check is believed, it is run against a machine that deliberately lacks the
            protection. If it does not fail there, it is not evidence. It is decoration.
          </div>
          <p>
            Every protection described in section 6 ships with a check that has been proven capable
            of returning a failure. A protection without one does not ship. This sounds obvious and
            it is the discipline most often skipped, including by us, which brings us to the
            uncomfortable part.
          </p>

          <h3>The register of our own failures</h3>
          <p>
            SP+ keeps a written register of twelve checks that passed while measuring nothing at all.
            Not bugs in the product: bugs in our own testing, each caught by the rule above and each
            written down rather than quietly corrected.
          </p>
          <div class="tablewrap">
            <table>
              <caption>Four families of check that proved nothing</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">What went wrong</th><th scope="col">Why it passed anyway</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>A check matched its own explanation</td>
                  <td>
                    It searched a file for a phrase, and found the phrase in the comment written
                    above the check describing what it was looking for.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>A check that could not fail</td>
                  <td>
                    Written so that every possible result counted as success. It reported green for
                    weeks without examining anything.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>A probe that never arrived</td>
                  <td>
                    It tested something, correctly, but not the thing it was aimed at, so the real
                    setting was never observed.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Reading that quietly corrupted the value</td>
                  <td>
                    The value was parsed wrongly on the way in, so a correct machine and an incorrect
                    one produced the same answer.
                  </td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            The most instructive one: a check confirmed a file sharing service was configured off,
            and printed a pass on every build, while that service was in fact listening to the
            network on the running machine. The configuration was right and the posture was wrong.
            The rule that came out of it now governs the whole suite, and it is the reason for the
            second layer of checking described above:
            <strong>measure the effect, never the text of the setting.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            We publish that register because a security document listing only successes tells you
            about its authors' confidence rather than about the system. If you are evaluating SP+
            with help from someone technical, that register is the right place for them to start.
          </p>

          <h3>What was measured on a real machine, not read off a plan</h3>
          <p>
            Encryption scheme and strength. Startup verification. That the engine's own protections
            are in force. That the file sharing service is not listening. That the password rule
            rejects an eleven-character password and accepts a long plain phrase. That exactly ten
            failed sign-ins trigger a lockout and the eleventh is not counted twice. That the updater
            cannot restart the machine on its own. That the software centre can no longer install
            system packages. That the assistant's notifier cannot escalate its own privileges. That a
            newly created file is readable by its owner and not by everyone.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="inside-card" id="s8" aria-labelledby="s8-title">
        <div class="inside-card-bar"><h2 id="s8-title">What SP+ deliberately does not do</h2><span>08</span></div>
        <div class="inside-body">
          <p>
            Each of these is a decision with a recorded reason and a recorded trigger that would
            reopen it. They are listed so that their absence is not mistaken for an oversight, and so
            that nobody later "fixes" one without understanding what it costs you.
          </p>
          <div class="tablewrap">
            <table>
              <caption>Rejected protections and the reason</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">Not done</th><th scope="col">Why not</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>Running the browser in a separate virtual machine</td>
                  <td>
                    The strongest answer to a browser compromise, and incompatible with one laptop
                    belonging to one non-technical person. Reopens if SP+ ever ships a managed second
                    machine.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Blocking unknown USB devices</td>
                  <td>
                    Docks, printers, keyboards, headsets and memory sticks all arrive by being
                    plugged in. This breaks the workday on day one.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Removing the classic administrator tools</td>
                  <td>
                    Some hardened systems delete them outright. That is exactly the class of change
                    that breaks printing or mounting a drive on a machine nobody can support. SP+
                    keeps an exact inventory of them instead, and a new one appearing fails the
                    build.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Turning off printer discovery</td>
                  <td>
                    Printing is the fourth thing SP+ must do on day one. A hardened system that
                    cannot find a printer gets replaced with an unhardened one.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Requiring a password for administrative actions</td>
                  <td>
                    The assistant cannot answer a prompt, the advisor often cannot either, and it
                    stops nobody who is already sitting at an unlocked machine. See the first item in
                    section 9, where this cost is stated in full.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Disabling a decade-old chip vulnerability class</td>
                  <td>
                    Costs up to 40% of the machine's speed, permanently, against a threat this user
                    does not face. Reopens only if that changes.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Replacing the browser</td>
                  <td>A settled decision. Security work hardens around Brave rather than relitigating it.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Rebuilding every component with extra hardening</td>
                  <td>
                    That turns SP+ into a Linux distribution rather than a careful layer on a
                    well-reviewed one, and a small team maintaining its own distribution is a worse
                    security outcome, not a better one.
                  </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Any claim of compliance, ever</td>
                  <td>See section 12. Reopens only with an actual audit, by actual auditors.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="inside-card" id="s9" aria-labelledby="s9-title">
        <div class="inside-card-bar"><h2 id="s9-title">What is still true after all of it</h2><span>09</span></div>
        <div class="inside-body">
          <p class="lead">
            Every product has this list. Most do not publish it. Read it as the counterweight to
            everything above, and bring it to whoever advises you on risk.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <strong>A laptop stolen while open and unlocked is fully compromised.</strong> The lock
              screen is the boundary, and it is the only boundary in that situation. Whoever is
              sitting at your unlocked machine has everything you have. Lock the screen when you
              stand up. The machine locks itself when the lid closes.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>If your browser is compromised, the whole machine follows.</strong> Code running
              as you reaches every client document and every saved credential. This is the strongest
              argument for the confinement work recorded in section 8, and the reason we say plainly
              that application confinement is this product's weakest layer.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Encrypted address lookups can be forced back to plain ones</strong> by an
              attacker who controls the network you joined.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Your hardware, its firmware and its startup keys are trusted and unverified by
              us.</strong> SP+ starts by trusting the machine you already own.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Going back a version is not a backup.</strong> There is no backup system in this
              release. A failed disk or ransomware means lost files. This is the gap most likely to
              affect an ordinary advisor in an ordinary year, and the one you should close yourself
              this week.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>SP+ is built on one machine, with no second party watching the process.</strong>
              A compromise of that machine reaches everything downstream of it. Moving the build to an
              independent, audited pipeline is recorded work and is not done.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Any program running as you can inspect any other program running as you</strong>,
              including the browser. Some of this is reduced in this release and none of it is
              eliminated.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Hostile documents are addressed only by the defaults of the office
              software</strong>, with nothing specific added.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>There is no formal machine-readable bill of materials</strong> in an
              industry-standard format, and no claim that our build is bit-for-bit reproducible.
              There is a complete component inventory and an exactly pinned foundation.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>You are part of the system.</strong> Phishing resistance rests on the browser's
              policy, the warning lists, and your own judgement on the day. No operating system
              changes that.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>SP+ does not defend against someone who has chosen you specifically</strong> and
              has time and budget to spend on it. It raises the cost of opportunistic compromise,
              which is what almost every real incident actually is.
            </li>
          </ol>
          <div class="note">
            <span class="tag">Not yet built</span>
            <p>
              A one-click Security Evidence Report, a readable summary of your machine's encryption,
              firewall, update history and significant events that you could attach to a cyber
              insurance application, is a designed and intended feature of SP+ and is not shipping
              yet. We mention it because you will hear about it, and because this document would be
              dishonest if it let you assume otherwise.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="inside-card" id="s10" aria-labelledby="s10-title">
        <div class="inside-card-bar"><h2 id="s10-title">A day with it</h2><span>10</span></div>
        <div class="inside-body">
          <h3>Getting it</h3>
          <p>
            Answer one question in the member portal: does Windows or a Mac feel more familiar? That
            decides your edition. Download it, and put it on any USB stick of 8 GB or more using our
            instructions. If you would rather not, it is a five-minute job for any capable helper.
          </p>

          <h3>Installing it</h3>
          <p>
            About twenty minutes, mostly unattended. You start the computer from the USB stick and
            answer prompts. You choose the code that unlocks the disk and the password you sign in
            with. You are shown the recovery key and asked to write it down, once. Then you remove
            the stick and restart onto a desktop that is already set up.
          </p>
          <p>
            Plan this like any computer change. Your web applications come across by signing in. Your
            files come from cloud storage or a backup. Anything Windows-only stays where it is. Keep
            your old machine or your old drive until you have confirmed everything you need is
            available.
          </p>

          <h3>The first hour</h3>
          <p>
            The assistant opens by itself and walks you through the handful of things that differ:
            where files go, how the browser is arranged, how to add a printer, what the lock screen
            does. Nothing is changed without your approval, including during the tour. Then you sign
            in to your accounts and add your printer.
          </p>

          <h3>Every day after</h3>
          <p>
            You work the way you already do. Email, calendar, proposals, illustrations, applications,
            e-signature, client video calls, all in the browser. Files save to your home folder, your
            cloud drives, or a USB drive. Close the lid and the screen locks.
          </p>

          <h3>When something goes wrong</h3>
          <p>
            Click the assistant and describe it in your own words. It checks the machine, tells you
            what it found in plain English, proposes a fix, and waits for you. It confirms afterwards
            that the fix worked. If it wants to make a change that would still be there next month,
            it says so first and you can decline.
          </p>

          <h3>Updates</h3>
          <p>
            They prepare themselves quietly and finish when you shut down. Restart whenever it suits:
            coffee, lunch, end of day. If a release ever misbehaves, the previous one is on the disk
            and the machine goes back to it. Your files are untouched in either direction.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="inside-card" id="s11" aria-labelledby="s11-title">
        <div class="inside-card-bar"><h2 id="s11-title">Questions advisors actually ask</h2><span>11</span></div>
        <div class="inside-body">
          <h4>Is it really free? What is the catch?</h4>
          <p>
            Free to eligible members, verified once through the member portal. No licence fee, no
            seat count, no premium tier. It is a member benefit, not a funnel into paid software. The
            catch, if you want one, is that it is a member benefit, so it goes away as a benefit if
            you leave, although your installed machine keeps working.
          </p>

          <h4>I have only ever used Windows. Will I be lost?</h4>
          <p>
            No. Choose the Windows-familiar edition and you get a taskbar, a start menu, and the
            click paths you know. Your work happens in the same websites as today.
          </p>

          <h4>What happens to my existing files and programs?</h4>
          <p>
            SP+ replaces the operating system, so treat it as a computer change. Web applications
            carry over by signing in, files come from cloud storage or a backup, and anything
            Windows-only that cannot move should stay on a machine that still runs Windows. Do not
            wipe your only computer on a Monday.
          </p>

          <h4>Can it read my client files?</h4>
          <p>
            The assistant works on the machine's technical state, not your documents, email, browsing
            history, passwords or client records, and what it may send for online diagnosis is
            limited by code to a list of technical facts. Screenshots go only with your approval, one
            at a time. Every request is recorded in the machine's own history where you can see it.
          </p>

          <h4>Will my printer work?</h4>
          <p>
            Almost certainly. Printers made in roughly the last decade speak a common standard that
            SP+ uses directly, with no driver hunting. Very old or unusual models are the exception.
            Test your printer during setup, before you rely on it.
          </p>

          <h4>What if an update breaks something?</h4>
          <p>
            The previous version is still on the disk and the machine goes back to it. Your files are
            unaffected either way.
          </p>

          <h4>Does this satisfy my compliance obligations?</h4>
          <p>
            No, and nothing else does either. SP+ gives you real controls and the ability to describe
            them honestly. Whether any particular requirement applies to you depends on your entity,
            your activities, your registrations and your regulators. Compliance stays yours. See
            section 12.
          </p>

          <h4>Who fixes it if something goes badly wrong?</h4>
          <p>
            Start with the built-in assistant. If the machine ever asks for your recovery key at
            startup, the guide explains exactly what happened and what to do, which is why we ask you
            to store that key properly on day one. Support sessions, where needed, are time-limited
            and require your approval.
          </p>

          <h4>What if SecureProspective stops making it?</h4>
          <p>
            Your machine keeps running. It is built on a mainstream Linux foundation rather than
            anything proprietary to us, and there is a written plan for discontinuation rather than
            an assumption that the question will not come up.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="inside-card" id="s12" aria-labelledby="s12-title">
        <div class="inside-card-bar"><h2 id="s12-title">Things we will never say to you</h2><span>12</span></div>
        <div class="inside-body">
          <p>
            This is an internal rule we publish on purpose. Each phrase below is banned from every
            SecureProspective page, email and conversation about SP+, and the right-hand column is
            what we say instead. If you ever hear one of the phrases on the left from us, we got it
            wrong and we would like to know.
          </p>
          <div class="tablewrap">
            <table>
              <caption>Banned language and its replacement</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Never</th><th scope="col">Because</th><th scope="col">Instead</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>"Compliant", "keeps you compliant"</td>
                  <td>We are not your auditor and cannot certify anything.</td>
                  <td>"Controls and records for your own compliance program"</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>"Meets FTC, NAIC or SEC requirements"</td>
                  <td>
                    Whether a rule applies depends on your entity and registrations, not on your
                    laptop.
                  </td>
                  <td>Describe the control itself</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>"Bank-grade", "military-grade", "unhackable"</td>
                  <td>Unverifiable superlatives.</td>
                  <td>"Strong encryption", "hardened defaults"</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>"Guaranteed to work with your devices"</td>
                  <td>No universal hardware guarantee exists before testing.</td>
                  <td>"Works with the vast majority of modern devices"</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>"Autonomous repair", "AI-approved fixes"</td>
                  <td>Overstates the assistant. Your approval is mandatory.</td>
                  <td>"Fixes only after you approve"</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>"We protect your client data"</td>
                  <td>We protect the computer. You protect the data.</td>
                  <td>"Built to reduce client-data risk"</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="inside-closing" aria-labelledby="closing-title">
        <h2 id="closing-title">The narrow claim we are actually making</h2>
        <p>
          Every protection listed in this document as shipping has been measured working on a real
          machine that was installed from a real USB stick and started up for the first time, and
          every one of those measurements has been shown capable of failing. Where a protection is
          absent, its absence is a decision with a written reason. Where a risk remains, it is in
          section 9.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is the whole claim. It is narrower than most security marketing and it is the only one
          worth making to someone who is about to trust us with the machine their clients' Social
          Security numbers live on.
        </p>
